      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this markdown. It's an OCR output of an OIML Certificate of Conformity PDF, but interestingly, the markdown itself contains what appears to be a GLM-style \"summary\" of page 2, plus the actual transcription of page 3.\n\nKey facts:\n- Certificate number: R76/2006-DK3-17.09\n- Issuing authority: DELTA, OIML Issuing Authority DK3 (Denmark)\n- Date of issue: 27 November 2017 → 2017-11-27\n- Test report: DELTA, DK, No. DANAK-1916850, dated 12-09-2016 (60 pages)\n- Signed by: J. Hovgård Jensen, Certification Manager\n- This is page 2 of 3, and page 3 of 3 — total pages: 3\n- Instrument: non-automatic weighing instrument under OIML R76/2006\n\nCharacteristics:\n- Accuracy class: III\n- Weighing range: Single-interval or dual-range\n- Maximum capacity (Max_i): 1.5 kg to 30 kg\n- Minimum capacity (Min_i): 20 e\n- Verification scale interval (e_i): ≥ 0.5 g\n- Number of intervals (Max_i / e_i): ≤ 6000 (single-interval); ≤ 3000 per range (dual-range)\n- Subtractive tare: ≤ −Max\n- Interface: Protective, according to paragraph 5.3.6\n- Supply voltage: 9 VDC from external adapter (100–240 VAC, 50–60 Hz); optional 6 V internal battery\n- Temperature range: −10 °C / +40 °C\n- Software identification: See page 3\n\nSoftware table (page 3):\n| MODEL NAME | APPLICATION | SOFTWARE IDENTIFICATION |\n- VGM, WEIGHING, CE020\n- VGW, CHECKWEIGHING, CE004\n- NGP, PRICE COMPUTING, CE005\n- VGC, PARTS COUNTING, CE006\n- VDM, WEIGHING, CE011\n- VDW, CHECKWEIGHING, CE012\n- VDP, PRICE COMPUTING, CE013\n- VDC, PARTS COUNTING, CE014\n- VOM, WEIGHING, CE007\n- VOWK, CHECKWEIGHING, CE008\n- VOP, PRICE COMPUTING, CE009\n- VOC, PARTS COUNTING, CE010\n\nCommon software version: 1.02xx, where xx = 00 to 99, verifiable via Fn1.\n\nAddress: DELTA, Venlighedsvej 4, 2970 Hørsholm, Denmark\nPhone: +45 7219 4000\nEmail: weighing@delta.dk\nWebsite: www.madebydelta.com\n\nNow let me build the JSON per schema.\n\ncertificate:\n- number: \"R76/2006-DK3-17.09\" — maybe canonical form \"DK3-17.09\" but keep as given: \"R76/2006-DK3-17.09\"\n- scheme: this is R76 under the old certification scheme... Actually OIML-CS has Scheme A and B. R76/2006 certificates issued by DK3... The old system certificates are typically \"Scheme A\" analog? Actually for OIML-CS: Scheme A = certificates issued with test reports by an issuing authority... Hmm. R76/2006 predates OIML-CS (which came in ~2017 for R76? Actually OIML CS applies to certain recommendations). This is a traditional OIML certificate. The schema says scheme (A|B). Since this is an old-style certificate, maybe omit or infer. The certificate number format \"R76/2006-DK3-17.09\" is the traditional OIML MAA format, not OIML-CS. I'll omit scheme since not present... Actually OIML-CS certificates have numbers like \"R76/2016-XX-CSA-yy.zz\". This one doesn't. I'll omit scheme or leave null. Rule 8: don't fabricate — omit.\n\n- project_number: not present.\n- page_total: 3\n- member_state: Denmark\n- date_issued: 2017-11-27\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"DK3\"\n\nissuing_authority:\n- name: \"DELTA\"\n- address_lines: [\"Venlighedsvej 4\", \"2970 Hørsholm\", \"Denmark\"]\n- person_responsible: \"J. Hovgård Jensen\"\n- person_title: \"Certification Manager\"\n- phone: \"+45 7219 4000\"\n- email: \"weighing@delta.dk\"\n- website: \"www.madebydelta.com\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"DK3\"\n\napplicants: not explicitly mentioned. The markdown doesn't name an applicant or manufacturer. Rule 8: omit. Hmm, but the instrument is a non-automatic weighing instrument... no manufacturer named. Omit both applicants and manufacturers.\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Indicator\" — the summary says \"(indicator)\". Actually it says \"non-automatic weighing instrument (indicator)\". So category: \"Indicator\" or \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\". The schema example categories include \"Indicator\". Given the text says indicator, use \"Indicator\".\n- type_designations: The model names from the software table: VGM, VGW, NGP, VGC, VDM, VDW, VDP, VDC, VOM, VOWK, VOP, VOC. These are model names of the certified type. Include all.\n- module_designation: Not applicable? Not stated. Omit or null. I'll set null? Schema allows null. Hmm, rule 8 says omit if not present. I'll omit... Actually the schema lists module_designation with \"Not applicable\" as an option. Since not mentioned, I'll omit.\n\nActually wait — should type_designations include all model names? The table is \"MODEL NAME\" — yes, these are model identifiers. Include.\n\nmodel_family: This cert covers multiple models (12 model names, and 3 families VG/VD/VO plus NGP). Hmm. Also characteristics vary: Max capacity 1.5 kg to 30 kg — is this per model? Not specified per model. The models in the software table are models with different applications. The \"weighing range\" attribute says \"Single-interval or dual-range\" — this varies by model? Not clear.\n\nI think a reasonable extraction: model_family with models list of the 12 model IDs, each with attributes: application, software_identification. And common_software_version as type_level characteristic.\n\nfamily_name: null.\n\nmodels: 12 entries with attributes {application, software_identification}.\n\ncharacteristics:\ntype_level:\n- accuracy_class: {value: \"III\", unit_symbol: null}\n- maximum_capacity: {value: {min: 1.5, max: 30}, unit_symbol: \"kg\"} — hmm, \"1.5 kg to 30 kg\" is a range across the family. Is this model_level? It's given as a range for the cert overall. Since we don't have per-model values, keep as type_level range. Actually per rule 4, varying attributes should be model_level, but we only have a family-level range, not per-model. Keep as type_level with range value.\n- minimum_capacity: {value: \"20 e\"}? Hmm. \"20 e\" — value 20 with unit... \"e\" is the verification scale interval, not a physical unit. Could express as value: 20, unit_symbol: \"e\". That's a reasonable approach: unit_symbol \"e\" (scale interval). I'll do value: 20, unit_symbol: \"e\".\n- verification_scale_interval: {value: {min: 0.5, max: null}, unit_symbol: \"g\"} (≥ 0.5 g)\n- max_number_of_scale_intervals: ≤ 6000 (single-interval); ≤ 3000 per range (dual-range). This is config-dependent → config_level with axis \"weighing_range_type\" (single-interval vs dual-range). Or type_level with two entries. Better: config_level:\n  {attribute: \"maximum_number_of_scale_intervals\", axis: \"weighing_range\", values: [{condition: \"single-interval\", value: {min: null, max: 6000}}, {condition: \"dual-range (per range)\", value: {min: null, max: 3000}}]}\n- subtractive_tare: \"≤ −Max\" — a formula/symbol. value: \"≤ -Max\"? Per rule: formulas/symbols keep as string. value: \"-Max\" with a max? It's \"≤ −Max\". I'll record value: {min: null, max: \"-Max\"}? That's odd. Better: value: \"-Max\" as string with a note... Hmm. Maybe record as {value: {min: null, max: \"-Max\"}, unit_symbol: null}? Hmm, \"-Max\" is a symbol not number. I think simplest: attribute subtractive_tare, value: \"≤ -Max\" as string. Actually the strict shape says value can be number | {min,max} | string. So value: \"-Max\", maybe unit null. I could add \"operator\" but schema doesn't allow. I'll use value: {min: null, max: \"-Max\"}? No — min/max should be numbers when used. Keep string: \"≤ -Max\". Hmm rule 2 says keep formulas as strings. So value: \"≤ -Max\"? The example \"Emax/14000\" kept without the ≤. I'll use value: \"-Max\" and... losing the ≤. Maybe value: {max: \"-Max\"}? Schema allows min/max values as number|string presumably. Rule 2: \"value\": <number | {min, max} | string>. It doesn't say min/max must be numbers. I'll do {min: null, max: \"-Max\"}? That seems acceptable but risky. I'll go with value: \"≤ -Max\" string. Hmm, but trailing... no footnote. Actually cleaner: attribute \"subtractive_tare\", value {min: null, max: \"-Max\"} — conveys \"up to -Max\". I think either fine. I'll choose value: \"-Max\", unit null. Eh. Let me just do value: {\"max\": \"-Max\", \"min\": null}? I'll go with the string \"≤ -Max\" — no wait, avoid symbols like ≤? The rule forbids LaTeX and footnote markers, not ≤. Example: `≤2.5m/s` → `{min: null, max: 2.5}`. So for \"-Max\" I can do min null max \"-Max\". I'll do that.\n\nActually simpler and safe: \"subtractive_tare\": {\"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": \"-Max\"}, \"unit_symbol\": null}. Fine.\n\n- interface: \"Protective, according to paragraph 5.3.6\" → attribute interface_type: value \"Protective\" plus maybe interface_reference: \"paragraph 5.3.6\". I'll do interface_type: {value: \"Protective, according to paragraph 5.3.6\"}. Or split: interface_type \"Protective\". Keep simple: one characteristic interface_type with value \"Protective (paragraph 5.3.6)\". Hmm, cleaner: value: \"Protective\". I'll include a second one? Rule says don't fabricate; splitting is fine. I'll just do interface_type: \"Protective, according to paragraph 5.3.6\" as string. Fine.\n\n- power supply: 9 VDC from external adapter (100–240 VAC, 50–60 Hz); optional 6 V internal battery.\n  Split per rule 3:\n  - supply_voltage_external_adapter: 9 V DC → value: 9, unit_symbol: \"V\", plus power_supply_type \"DC\"? And adapter input 100–240 VAC → power_supply_voltage_adapter_input {min 110... no, 100–240}, unit \"V\"; frequency 50–60 Hz.\n  - battery_voltage: 6 V.\n  \n  Let me define:\n  - \"supply_voltage\": {value: 9, unit_symbol: \"V\"} — with note it's DC. Maybe supply_voltage_type: \"DC\".\n  - \"external_adapter_input_voltage\": {value: {min: 100, max: 240}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}, type AC.\n  - \"external_adapter_input_frequency\": {value: {min: 50, max: 60}, unit_symbol: \"Hz\"}\n  - \"battery_voltage\": {value: 6, unit_symbol: \"V\"}, optional. Could add footnote-like marker? There's \"optional\". I'll add attributes power_supply_type: \"AC\" for adapter, \"DC\" for main supply. And battery: \"optional\" — maybe include in value string? Keep it simple: battery_voltage_optional: {value: 6, unit_symbol: \"V\"}. Hmm attribute naming: \"optional_internal_battery_voltage\". I'll do that.\n\n- temperature_range: {value: {min: -10, max: 40}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n\n- common_software_version: value: \"1.02xx\" with note xx = 00–99. Could be value \"1.02xx\" and a characteristic common_software_version_range? I'll do common_software_version: {value: \"1.02xx\"} and maybe footnote/notes aren't in schema. Add characteristic \"common_software_version_suffix\": {value: {min: 0, max: 99}}? That may be over-engineering. I'll add description in matrix or just keep \"1.02xx (xx = 00–99)\"? Keep value: \"1.02xx\" and another characteristic \"common_software_version_variable_suffix_range\": value {min:0, max:99}. Hmm. I'll include one: common_software_version: value \"1.02xx\", and model... Actually the table plus the version note could go into matrix_tables. The software table fits matrix_tables nicely: name \"Approved software identification by model\", columns [model_name, application, software_identification], rows 12.\n\nconfig_level: number of intervals per weighing range type — as discussed.\n\nmatrix_tables:\n- name: \"Approved software identification by model\"\n- columns: [\"model_name\", \"application\", \"software_identification\"]\n- rows: 12 objects.\n\ntest_reports:\n- id: \"DANAK-1916850\", date: 2016-09-12, pages: 60, role: \"type_evaluation_report\". The date \"12-09-2016\" — Danish format DD-MM-YYYY → 12 September 2016 → \"2016-09-12\".\n\nrecommendation:\n- id: \"R76\", edition: 2006, accuracy_classes: [\"III\"]. scheme: not stated (traditional cert, not OIML-CS scheme A/B). The schema includes scheme in recommendation. Omit since unknown.\n\nrevision_history: none present. Omit.\n\nfootnotes: none. Omit.\n\npage_total: 3 (page 2 of 3, page 3 of 3, certificate includes 3 pages).\n\nmember_state: \"Denmark\" — DK3 is Denmark. Yes.\n\ndate_issued: \"2017-11-27\".\n\nNow certificate.number: \"R76/2006-DK3-17.09\".\n\nLet me also double check model_level characteristics: Should I create model_level entries for application and software_identification? Rule 4 says varying attributes → model_level. Application varies per model, software ID varies per model. I could add model_level entries for \"application\" and \"software_identification\". That plus models[].attributes would be redundant. The schema example puts attributes in models[] entries. I'll put application and software_identification in each model's attributes, and skip model_level for those (to avoid redundancy). That seems fine.\n\nmodels[]:\n- {\"model_id\": \"VGM\", \"label\": \"VGM\", \"attributes\": {\"application\": \"WEIGHING\", \"software_identification\": \"CE020\"}}\netc. label maybe omit or same as model_id. I'll include label only where meaningful; maybe omit label. Schema shows label in the shape but it's fine to include. I'll include label equal to model name for clarity? To be safe, include \"label\" with application? E.g. label: \"VGM (WEIGHING)\". Hmm, keep simple: omit label. Schema: models[]: {model_id, label, attributes{}}. Omitting label is fine per rule 8? Label isn't \"present\" explicitly. I'll omit.\n\nCertificate category: \"Indicator\". Hmm — the summary says \"non-automatic weighing instrument (indicator)\". Model names like VGM (weighing), NGP (price computing) suggest complete weighing instruments though (platform scales). But we go with the doc: indicator. Actually the doc parenthetically says \"(indicator)\". I'll use category \"Non-automatic weighing instrument (indicator)\"? Schema example categories: \"Indicator\". I'll use \"Indicator\" and description: \"Non-automatic weighing instrument under OIML R 76/2006\". Hmm, description free-text if present. The markdown says \"This certificate applies to a non-automatic weighing instrument (indicator) under the OIML R76/2006 standard.\" That's a GLM summary line though. Still it's content. I'll include description: \"Non-automatic weighing instrument (indicator)\".\n\nWait — actually \"weighing range: Single-interval or dual-range\" — this varies across models/configs. Could be config axis \"range_type\": single-interval vs multi-interval(dual-range). Already captured in config_level for number of intervals. Also could add config_level entry for \"weighing_range_type\" values [\"single-interval\", \"dual-range\"]? That's the axis itself, not a characteristic. Skip.\n\nAccuracy class III — type_level.\n\nMax capacity range 1.5–30 kg: type_level with range. Good.\n\nNow write JSON.\n\nCharacteristics structured values need unit_id? Leave null unless certain. I can set unit_id for °C: \"u:degree_Celsius\" is given as example of known ID. \"kg\": \"u:kilogram\". \"V\": \"u:volt\". \"Hz\": \"u:hertz\". \"g\": \"u:gram\". Rule says \"Leave unit_id: null unless you are certain of the unitsdb ID.\" The examples show u:degree_Celsius, u:kilogram presumably valid. I'll fill in certain ones: u:degree_Celsius, u:kilogram, u:gram, u:volt, u:hertz. \"e\" (scale interval) — not a unit, null. \"t\" not used here. \"%\" not used.\n\nHmm, risk: being \"certain\". The examples in the rules give u:degree_Celsius explicitly. I'll use those standard ones.\n\nLet me finalize attributes naming:\n- accuracy_class\n- maximum_capacity (Max_i) — canonical: maximum_capacity. Unit kg, value {min:1.5, max:30}.\n- minimum_capacity — value 20, unit \"e\" (scale interval). unit_symbol: \"e\", unit_id null.\n- verification_scale_interval — {min: 0.5, max: null}, \"g\".\n- maximum_number_of_scale_intervals — config_level.\n- subtractive_tare\n- interface_type\n- supply_voltage (9 V DC)\n- power_supply_type: \"DC\" (external adapter output?) hmm. Main supply: 9 VDC from external adapter. Adapter input: 100–240 VAC 50–60 Hz.\n  - \"supply_voltage\": 9 V (DC)\n  - \"power_supply_type\": \"DC\"\n  - \"external_adapter_input_voltage\": {100–240} V (AC)\n  - \"external_adapter_input_frequency\": {50–60} Hz\n  - \"optional_internal_battery_voltage\": 6 V\n- temperature_range: -10 to 40 °C\n- common_software_version: \"1.02xx\"\n\nconfig_level:\n[\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"maximum_number_of_scale_intervals\",\n    \"axis\": \"weighing range configuration\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"condition\": \"single-interval\", \"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 6000}, \"unit_symbol\": null},\n      {\"condition\": \"dual-range (per range)\", \"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 3000}, \"unit_symbol\": null}\n    ]\n  }\n]\n\nNote the structured form for config values: {condition, value, unit_symbol}. The value should be structured object too? The schema shows values[]: {condition, value}. Rule 2 says every characteristic value must be object with value/unit_symbol. I'll include unit_symbol: null.\n\nmatrix_tables rows: 12 rows as extracted.\n\nAlso footnotes: none.\n\ntest_reports date: \"2016-09-12\".\n\ncertificate.date_issued: \"2017-11-27\".\n\noiml_issuer_id: \"DK3\".\n\nscheme: omit (not an OIML-CS scheme cert). Actually the schema says scheme (A|B). This certificate predates/is outside OIML-CS. Omit.\n\nLet me now also consider: should type_designations include \"VGM\" etc. plus maybe family prefixes VG, VD, VO? The table lists 12 model names. Include those 12.\n\nWrite final JSON.",
